Oral and intravenous (IV) contrast-enhanced axial computed tomography images showing a well-defined polypoidal mass lesion (
arrow
) arising from the posterior wall of the descending colon with a mass lesion involving the muscularis propria and no evidence of involvement of pericolonic tissue, suggestive of a T2 lesion.

Contrast-enhanced axial computed tomography images showing a large circumferential well-defined heterogeneously enhancing centrally necrotic infiltrating mass lesion causing involvement of pericolonic tissue and infiltration into adjacent small bowel loops (
arrow
), suggestive of a T4 lesion.

Portal phase axial contrast-enhanced computed tomography scan image through the liver demonstrates an ill-defined poorly enhancing hypodense lesion in the right lobe of the liver (
arrow
) in a known case of carcinoma of the sigmoid colon, suggesting liver metastasis.

Axial T2-weighted magnetic resonance image showing a normal anatomy of the rectum, mesorectal fat (*), and mesorectal fascia (
arrow
).
